# platform.linux.storage.device

mm.mcu on the storage-linux boards: platform.linux.storage.bus over a virtual
SD card and a virtual SPI flash, each kept in an image file the device map
names -- sdcard.path, sdcard.size, sdcard.kind, spiflash.path, spiflash.size,
docs/modules-platform-linux.mdy has the keys -- and sdcard.img and
spiflash.img in the working directory when it names none. The files are
opened at the first mm.mcu call, not at load, so the map is resolved after
every provider has registered.

A missing file is made at its size, the card's zero-filled as a new card
reads and the flash's 0xFF as an erased chip reads; one that exists keeps its
contents and its own size, so an image made with mkfs.fat or littlefs-python
is used as it is. A file that cannot be opened, an SD image whose size the
card kind cannot describe, a flash image whose size is not a power of two
from 64 KiB to 16 MiB, or a device map that did not resolve leaves the chip
absent -- it never answers, as an empty socket does not -- with one line on
standard error saying why.
